<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munmu of Silla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/626">626</a>–<a href="/years/681">681</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Munmu was king of Silla from 661 to 681, son of King Muyeol. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> he completed the conquest of Baekje and destroyed Goguryeo in 668, and then fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silla-tang-war">Silla–Tang War</a>, which ended in 676 with Silla controlling the peninsula south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a>. He asked to be buried at sea to guard the kingdom as a dragon, and his tomb is placed at Daewangam rock.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munmu-of-silla.jpg" alt="Munmu of Silla" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christophe95 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_kings_at_Tongiljeon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Munmu was born as Kim Pŏmmin and took the name Munmu when he succeeded his father, King Muyeol. His mother, Munmyeong, was the younger sister of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a>. Under his father he held the office of pajinchan, which apparently was responsible for maritime affairs, and he played a key part in developing the kingdom's diplomatic links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>. He is usually considered the first ruler of the Unified Silla period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Munmu took the throne in the midst of a long conflict with Baekje and Goguryeo, soon after General Kyebaek and the Baekje army had been defeated at Sabi by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a> in 660. Silla was heavily aided by the Tang in these struggles. The first years of the reign went on trying to defeat Goguryeo, after an abortive attempt in 661. In 667 he ordered a further attack, which led to the fall of Goguryeo in 668.</p><p>After Goguryeo fell, Tang created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate-general-to-pacify-the-east">Protectorate General to Pacify the East</a> and tried to place the whole peninsula, Silla included, under its rule. Munmu allied with Goguryeo resistance leaders such as Geom Mojam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anseung">Anseung</a> and attacked the Tang forces in the former Baekje lands. By 674 Silla and its former ally were in constant battle. Emperor Gaozong, in anger, declared Munmu's brother Kim In-mun to be king, but Munmu apologised formally and offered tribute, and Gaozong recalled Kim In-mun. In 675 Silla defeated Tang forces led by Li Jinxing, who had arrived with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> troops, at the Maeso fortress. Gaozong then ordered all Tang forces to leave the peninsula and moved the Protectorate General to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Munmu ruled the unified kingdom for twenty years and fell ill in 681. On his deathbed he left a will and passed the crown to his son, Prince Sinmun, asking that his body be cremated and the ashes scattered in the sea where the whales live, so that he might become a dragon and thwart foreign invasion. Sinmun built the Gomun Temple in his honour, a waterway for the sea dragon to pass between sea and land, and a pavilion overlooking the islet of Daewangam, where later kings could pay their respects.</p><p>Many modern Korean scholars, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, doubt that Silla was the first state to unify the Korean people, since it did not conquer parts of Goguryeo and Balhae in the northeast; some regard Goryeo as the first. Silla's victory over Tang is generally regarded as a critical turning point in Korean history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Munmu of Silla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King <b>Munmu</b>, who completed Silla's unification, is said to have asked for burial at sea so that he could become a dragon guarding Korea; a rock islet off the coast near Gyeongju is held to be his tomb.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.85,129.22&amp;year=661" aria-label="Gyeongju on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Munmu of Silla"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5636 4475h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5636 4475h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.85,129.22&amp;year=661">What was here in 661 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
